F={}
F["cannabis-concentrates"]={
"onset":["Cannabis concentrates (wax, shatter, budder, rosin, live resin, distillate in vape cartridges) contain far more THC than flower. Inhaled, they act within seconds: a sudden rush of heat to the head and face, a fast, pounding heart, dry mouth, red eyes and a heavy, tingling sensation spreading from the scalp down the limbs. Harsh vapor can make people cough hard.","Mentally the onset is fast and intense. A rush of euphoria or giddiness often arrives with racing thoughts, distorted time and heightened senses; for people used to flower, the strength can be startling. Some feel panic rising just as quickly as the high.","Because a single inhalation delivers so much THC, inexperienced people are more likely to have anxiety attacks, fainting or vomiting. The pounding heart can be risky for those with heart conditions, and vape cartridges from unregulated sources have been linked to serious lung injury (EVALI)."],
"peak":["At the peak the body feels very heavy or melted into the seat, with a strong pulse, dry mouth, red eyes and sometimes dizziness when standing. Coordination and reaction time are clearly impaired, and some people feel cold, shaky or nauseated.","Mentally the peak can be intensely euphoric, dreamy or introspective, with music and sensations magnified and time slowed to a crawl. It can also tip into paranoia, a sense of losing control, racing heart worries or feeling detached from reality.","High-potency use is linked to greater risk of psychosis, especially in young people and those with a family history of psychotic illness. Fainting and falls, and driving impairment, are immediate dangers."],
"plateau":["The plateau lasts an hour or two after inhaling. The body stays heavy and relaxed, the eyes droop, appetite often surges and the mouth stays dry. Some people drift toward sleep on the couch.","Mentally thoughts are slow, wandering and easily distracted, short-term memory is poor and conversations lose their thread. Anxiety may ease into calm or linger as unease.","Frequent use of concentrates drives tolerance quickly and is linked to cannabis use disorder and cannabis hyperemesis syndrome, a condition of repeated severe vomiting that is relieved only by stopping."],
"comedown":["As it wears off, people feel tired, foggy, thirsty and hungry, sometimes with a mild headache. Sleep may come easily but feel less restful, with fewer dreams.","Mentally there can be lingering slowness and low motivation. Some feel anxious or flat, especially after an overwhelming session.","Daily heavy users who stop often experience irritability, poor sleep, vivid dreams, low appetite and craving for one to two weeks. Support and gradually cutting down help."]}
F["cannabis-edibles"]={
"onset":["Edibles (gummies, chocolates, baked goods, drinks) deliver THC through the gut, so the onset is slow, often taking from half an hour to two hours or more. The first signs are subtle: a warm heaviness in the limbs, a slight tingle, dry mouth and then a growing pulse in the chest. The liver converts THC into a stronger, longer-lasting form, so effects can build much higher than expected.","Mentally the onset often begins with uncertainty and then a gradual or sudden arrival of euphoria, giggles, distorted time and a dreamy, floating mind. Many people mistakenly think the edible isn't working and take more, only for both portions to hit together.","This delayed, stacking effect is the most common reason for edible emergencies, including severe anxiety, vomiting, fainting and racing heart. Edibles that look like candy are a serious poisoning risk for children and pets."],
"peak":["At the peak, a few hours in, the body may feel very heavy, sedated and sunk into bed or couch, with a racing or pounding heart, dry mouth, red eyes and sometimes nausea, chills or dizziness on standing.","Mentally the peak is often more intense and body-centered than smoking: strong euphoria, deep relaxation, vivid sensations or, for many, a frightening sense of being trapped in a high that won't end, paranoia and confusion about time.","Children who eat edibles may become very drowsy, unsteady or unresponsive and need urgent medical care. Adults with heart disease may be at risk from the raised heart rate."],
"plateau":["The plateau is long, often several hours. The body stays heavy and slow, appetite can be strong, and some people fall asleep for many hours.","Mentally thinking is slowed and memory poor; some feel peaceful, others anxious that the effect will never stop. Reassurance that it will fade helps.","Driving or using machinery is unsafe for the full duration, which can stretch most of the day."],
"comedown":["The comedown can last into the next day, with grogginess, dry mouth and a heavy, foggy head sometimes called an 'edible hangover.'","Mentally there may be slowness, low motivation and mild anxiety or embarrassment after an overwhelming experience.","Storing edibles locked away from children and pets, and waiting fully before taking more, prevent most emergencies."]}
F["delta-8-thc"]={
"onset":["Delta-8-THC is a close chemical cousin of regular THC (delta-9), usually made by converting hemp-derived CBD and sold in gas stations, vape shops and online, often in gummies and vape cartridges. Inhaled it acts within minutes; eaten, it can take an hour or more. The first sensations are a gentle warmth, dry mouth, red eyes and a light, relaxing heaviness.","Mentally the onset is often described as a milder, clearer, less anxious high than regular THC: relaxed, gently euphoric and a little spacey, though in larger amounts it can feel very similar.","Because products are loosely regulated, labels can be wrong, and byproducts of the chemical conversion may be present. Poison centers have reported many cases of children eating delta-8 gummies."],
"peak":["At the peak the body feels relaxed and heavy, with a faster heart, dry mouth and impaired coordination. Some feel sleepy, dizzy or nauseated.","Mentally there is calm, euphoria, slowed time and heightened senses, with impaired short-term memory. Anxiety and paranoia are less common than with strong delta-9 but still possible.","Delta-8 impairs driving and can cause positive drug tests for THC."],
"plateau":["The plateau lasts a couple of hours inhaled and longer eaten. The body stays relaxed, appetite may increase, and drowsiness grows.","Mentally thoughts drift and focus is poor; many feel content and mellow.","Frequent use builds tolerance and can lead to dependence like regular cannabis."],
"comedown":["The comedown brings tiredness, dry mouth and some grogginess, especially after edibles.","Mentally there may be lingering fogginess and low motivation.","Regular users who stop may notice irritability, poor sleep and craving for a week or two."]}
F["synthetic-cannabinoids"]={
"onset":["Synthetic cannabinoids ('Spice', 'K2', 'mamba', and newer names) are lab-made chemicals sprayed on plant material or paper, or put in vape liquid, that act on cannabis receptors far more strongly than THC. Smoked, they hit within seconds: a sudden heavy rush, racing heart, flushing, dry mouth and often a strange, numbing pressure in the head.","Mentally the onset is abrupt and unpredictable. Some feel euphoria and relaxation, but many describe a harsh, disorienting jolt, with anxiety, confusion and a sense of the world going wrong.","Because the chemicals keep changing and are unevenly sprayed, two hits from the same packet can differ greatly in strength. Hospitals have seen clusters of poisonings, including seizures, heart attacks, kidney injury and deaths."],
"peak":["At the peak the body may become limp and unresponsive, or tense and agitated. A very fast heart, chest pain, vomiting, sweating and seizures can occur. People may collapse in a zombie-like stupor in public places.","Mentally there can be intense paranoia, terror, hallucinations, psychosis or a blank, detached state with little memory afterward.","Severe agitation, overheating, seizures, heart rhythm problems and stopped breathing (especially mixed with opioids or alcohol) are medical emergencies. Some batches have been contaminated with rat poison, causing severe bleeding."],
"plateau":["The plateau is often short but chaotic, and many people redose frequently. The body stays wrung-out, nauseated and clumsy.","Mentally confusion, paranoia and mood swings continue, sometimes with violent or self-harming behavior.","Use is especially common in prisons and homeless communities, where it is cheap and hard to detect."],
"comedown":["The comedown brings exhaustion, nausea, headache and sweating.","Mentally there can be deep anxiety, low mood and craving.","Withdrawal after regular use can be severe, with vomiting, sweating, insomnia and agitation; medical support helps."]}
F["dronabinol-nabilone"]={
"onset":["Dronabinol (Marinol, Syndros) is a prescription form of THC, and nabilone (Cesamet) is a synthetic relative. They are prescribed for nausea from chemotherapy, appetite loss in HIV/AIDS and sometimes pain. Taken as capsules, they act slowly, over half an hour to a couple of hours, starting with drowsiness, a dry mouth and a mild heaviness.","Mentally the onset can be subtle, with relaxation and easing of nausea, or, in some people, a 'high' with euphoria, dizziness and a dreamy mind. Older adults and people new to THC may feel it strongly.","Because effects are delayed, taking extra early can stack into an uncomfortable experience. Doctors usually start low and increase slowly."],
"peak":["At their peak, the body feels relaxed, sleepy and sometimes dizzy, with a faster heart, dry mouth and low blood pressure on standing. Appetite often rises and nausea eases.","Mentally there may be euphoria, calm, mild confusion or, in some, anxiety, paranoia or mood changes. Memory and concentration are impaired.","Falls in older people and driving impairment are key concerns. People with a history of psychosis or severe heart disease need special caution."],
"plateau":["The plateau lasts several hours. The body stays drowsy and relaxed, appetite continues and nausea stays improved.","Mentally thinking may be slow and foggy, and mood generally calm.","Misuse potential is lower than smoked cannabis because of the slow onset, but it exists."],
"comedown":["As it fades, people may feel tired and groggy, with a dry mouth.","Mentally there may be mild fogginess.","Stopping after long, regular use can cause irritability, poor sleep and loss of appetite for a short time."]}
F["thc-o"]={
"onset":["THC-O acetate is a semi-synthetic THC compound made from hemp-derived chemicals and sold online and in shops as vapes and gummies. It is marketed as stronger and more 'psychedelic' than THC. Eaten, it can take a long time to begin; inhaled, it comes on faster. Early effects include heaviness, dry mouth, red eyes and a warm tingle.","Mentally the onset can be slow to recognize and then strong, with euphoria, dreaminess and distorted time; some describe mild visual changes.","Its strength and slow onset make accidental overconsumption likely. Heating acetate compounds in vapes may create a harmful gas (ketene) that can damage the lungs, a concern also linked to vitamin E acetate in vaping lung injury."],
"peak":["At the peak the body feels very heavy and sedated, with a fast heart, dizziness and sometimes nausea or vomiting.","Mentally there can be intense euphoria, deep relaxation or strong anxiety, paranoia and confusion, similar to a very strong THC edible.","Regulation is weak and products are often untested; the US DEA has stated THC-O is not legal hemp."],
"plateau":["The plateau is long. The body stays sedated and appetite may increase.","Mentally thinking stays slow and memory poor.","Driving is unsafe for many hours."],
"comedown":["The comedown can include grogginess and a heavy head into the next day.","Mentally fogginess and low motivation may linger.","Regular use builds tolerance and dependence like other THC products."]}
F["hhc"]={
"onset":["HHC (hexahydrocannabinol) is a hydrogenated form of THC made from hemp-derived chemicals and sold in vapes, gummies and flower in many countries. Inhaled it comes on within minutes; eaten, over an hour or more. Early effects include warmth, relaxation, dry mouth and red eyes.","Mentally many describe it as similar to regular THC, somewhere between delta-8 and delta-9 in strength: gently euphoric, relaxed and slightly spacey.","Products vary a lot in strength and purity. Several European countries banned HHC after it spread quickly."],
"peak":["At the peak the body is relaxed and heavy, with a faster heart and impaired coordination.","Mentally there is euphoria, slowed time and heightened senses; anxiety and paranoia can occur at higher amounts.","Driving is impaired, and drug tests may detect it."],
"plateau":["The plateau lasts a few hours. Appetite may increase and drowsiness grows.","Mentally thoughts drift and focus is poor.","Tolerance builds with frequent use."],
"comedown":["The comedown brings tiredness and dry mouth.","Mentally mild fogginess may linger.","Regular users who stop may feel irritable and sleep poorly for a while."]}
for k,v in F.items():
    if k in D: D[k].setdefault("feel",{}).update(v)
A={
"cannabis-concentrates":{"plateau":["Red eyes and a dry, sticky mouth persist, and standing up quickly may bring a rush of dizziness.","Some people find themselves staring at one thing for long stretches or laughing at small things.","Tolerance built on concentrates also makes ordinary flower feel weak."],
"comedown":["The eyes may feel heavy and dry, and the throat raw from harsh vapor.","Some feel unsettled by fragments of anxious thoughts from the peak, or vaguely guilty about lost time.","Repeated vomiting episodes with cramping, eased by hot showers, are a warning sign of hyperemesis syndrome."]},
"cannabis-edibles":{"peak":["Some feel cold and shivery, others hot and sweaty, and the limbs can feel too heavy to lift.","","A calm room, sips of water, a snack and a trusted person usually help; poison control can advise."],
"plateau":["The mouth stays dry, the eyes heavy, and getting up may bring dizziness. Waves of the high may roll in and out, and some people feel sick and need to lie still.","Some find music, films or food deeply absorbing; others feel stuck in loops of worry, checking their pulse or watching the clock.","Mixing with alcohol can worsen nausea and dizziness and may lead to vomiting."],
"comedown":["Some wake with a stiff body, sticky mouth and a lingering sense of being slowed, which can last until midday. Appetite may stay high.","Some feel detached or not quite themselves for a while, which usually resolves with sleep, food and time.","For adults, slow and patient experience with edibles is the main safeguard; panic during an overdose usually passes, but chest pain or fainting needs care."]},
"delta-8-thc":{"peak":["Eyes become red and heavy, and the mouth is dry. Some people notice their heart thumping and feel slightly lightheaded on standing. Edible forms can feel heavier and more sedating than vapes.","Some find it easier to stay socially comfortable than with regular THC, while others notice slow speech and losing track of thoughts mid-sentence.","Products often contain unlabeled amounts of delta-9 THC or other cannabinoids, so effects may be stronger than expected."],
"plateau":["Muscles feel loose, the eyes droop, and lying down feels very comfortable. Some people feel a mild headache or dizziness, especially after vaping a lot.","Some people enjoy music, food or films more; others feel foggy and want to sleep. Short-term memory remains poor and time feels slow.","Children who eat delta-8 gummies can become very sleepy or unresponsive and need medical care."],
"comedown":["Some wake the next day slightly groggy, with dry eyes and mouth, especially after gummies taken late. Appetite may stay high.","Some feel slightly slow, unmotivated or forgetful for some hours. Anxiety after a strong experience usually fades with rest.","Daily heavy vaping can build dependence much like regular cannabis; cutting down gradually helps."]},
"synthetic-cannabinoids":{"peak":["","Some people describe feeling as if they were dying or had left their body.",""],
"plateau":["Heart rate stays high, and vomiting and sweating can recur. Muscle twitches, slurred speech and difficulty walking are common, and some people lie unresponsive for long stretches.","Some feel numb and empty, others jittery and suspicious, and moods can change quickly. Memory of the episode is often patchy.","Someone who collapses should be placed on their side and emergency services called; naloxone is worth giving if opioids may also be involved."],
"comedown":["Muscles may ache and the chest feel tight; appetite is poor and the stomach unsettled. Sleep may be broken by sweats and vivid dreams. Kidney and heart problems can appear in the following days.","Some feel deeply ashamed or frightened by what happened, and paranoid thoughts can linger for days. Daily users often feel desperate for the next dose within hours.","Mental-health and addiction services, and housing support, can make a real difference for people caught in daily use."]},
"dronabinol-nabilone":{"peak":["Some feel unsteady when walking or standing up quickly, and eyes may be red. Nausea relief can be very welcome for chemotherapy patients.","Some patients find the high unpleasant or disorienting and prefer lower doses; others feel calmer and less distressed by their illness.",""],
"plateau":["Sleepiness may deepen, and some people rest or nap. Dizziness on standing may continue, and the mouth stays dry. Eating often becomes easier.","Mild confusion or slowed thinking may continue, and some people notice changes in mood, such as feeling unusually cheerful or flat. Memory for this period may be hazy.","Patients should not drive or operate machinery until they know how the medicine affects them, and should avoid alcohol."],
"comedown":["Grogginess may linger into the next morning, especially if taken late in the day. Balance may be off for some hours, so older adults should take care with stairs.","Some feel slightly flat, unmotivated or forgetful; others feel rested. Any troubling mood changes, confusion or hallucinations should be reported to the prescriber.","Doctors may adjust timing, such as taking it at night, to reduce daytime effects."]},
"thc-o":{"peak":["Some feel chills, a racing pulse and an urge to lie still, and coordination becomes very poor. Some people feel numb or tingly.","Some report a dreamlike, slightly psychedelic feel, with vivid inner imagery and altered sense of space, but many simply feel very high and sleepy.","Calm surroundings, water and time help; chest pain, fainting or severe vomiting need medical care."],
"plateau":["Heaviness and sleepiness continue, with a dry mouth and red eyes. Some people feel queasy and dizzy, especially if they took more while waiting for the onset. Lying down helps.","Concentration is very limited, and some people feel stuck in an extended dreamy state that seems to last forever. Anxiety may rise and fall in waves.","Products are often untested, and contents may differ from labels."],
"comedown":["Many people wake feeling slow, thirsty and heavy-limbed, with a mild headache, especially after gummies. Appetite may stay high.","Thinking can remain sluggish for much of the next day, and some feel a little low or anxious after an overly strong experience. Rest and food help.","Lung symptoms such as cough or shortness of breath after vaping should be checked by a doctor."]},
"hhc":{"onset":["Some feel a light pressure behind the eyes and a gently racing pulse.","","Some products also contain other semi-synthetic cannabinoids."],
"peak":["Eyes become red and dry, the mouth sticky, and some people feel lightheaded on standing. Muscles loosen and the body feels warm and heavy; some notice a strong pulse.","Some find it pleasantly mellow and social; others feel distracted and lose the thread of conversations. Time can seem to stretch.","Driving is impaired, and drug tests may detect it. Higher amounts or strong vapes can cause anxiety, dizziness and nausea, especially in new users."],
"plateau":["The body stays relaxed and slowed, with dry eyes and mouth and a mild pulse increase. Some people feel sleepy and lie down; edibles make the plateau longer and heavier.","Thoughts drift, short-term memory is poor, and some people enjoy music or food more. Others feel foggy and want to rest.","Tolerance builds with frequent use, and the same amount feels weaker over time."],
"comedown":["The comedown brings tiredness, dry mouth and sometimes a mild headache. After edibles, grogginess can last into the next day.","Mild fogginess, low motivation and forgetfulness may linger for a few hours. Some feel calm, others a little flat.","Regular users who stop may feel irritable, sleep poorly and have vivid dreams for a week or two."]},
}
